spam@jcatena.net wrote in message news:<87zn5amc2m.fsf@ash.jcatena.net>...
> Hi all. I'm writing a patch to implement species allies and species enemies
> for Rangers.  When a Ranger is created, one monster race is selected randomly
> to be the Ranger's species ally, and another monster race is selected to be
> the species enemy.

That is a very cool idea. I like rangers and I'll add my comments, but
I'm not a programmer so I don't know how implementable my ideas would
be...

> PATCH SPOILER:
> 
> Does anyone have other suggestions for what effects this patch should have?
> So far, the species enemy will be untameable (taming has no effect).  The
> species ally will be peaceful when created, so it will not normally attack
> first, but will counterattack.
> 
> A few ideas I've rejected as too obvious and unbalancing: Ranger starts with a
> tamed monster of the ally species; Ranger auto-tames them; they never attack;
> enemy species is always hostile, stalking, or attacks.

Auto-tame is too unbalancing, but surely perhaps something that would
improve the success rate of a charm/tame effect? (You probably already
have this in place)

Could it be possible to tame the allied-creature by feeding them, much
as you can do with cats, dogs, and horses? Perhaps limit the kind of
food that can be used to tame them (e.g. in the way that horses will
only eat veggies or fungi), if this is too unbalancing?

Could you #chat with the allied creature and get some kind of tame
bonus or helpful hint (as if you had animal empathy)?

If not tame them, could species allies act like your comrades on your
quest plane--i.e. fighting the monsters that are attacking you, but
they don't join you either?

Like D&D, would the ranger get a bonus to hit, damage, and detection
of his species enemy that increases as he gains in level?

When the ranger reaches a certain level, could he be automatically
warned to the presence of his favored enemy (the way Sting makes you
aware of orcs)?

Or is this basically--some monsters will be peaceful whereas they
normally wouldn't, and others will always be hostile? If that's the
case--is the effect really balancing? There are a lot of creatures
that will probably always be hostile anyway, so in effect you're just
gaining the species ally. Thus, I think it's going to be more
effective if you give the ranger a little more than always
peaceful/always hostile.

> I can't release a beta patch right now: I haven't yet coded the random
> selection of races (which should be excluded?), 

Any @ or & should be excluded on both counts, I think. Possibly D as
well. F, j, and most bs would make fairly useless allies, esp. if it's
easier to tame them, because they don't move or are so slow.

Would it be better to exclude all sentient races on both counts?

In D&D--which is where I assume the idea came from--the ranger gets
his special animal companion, but can choose favored enemy from all
types (and in the new version of the game, from whole classes of
monsters, not just a specific one). If you make it like that, I'd say
let the favored ally be only from nonsentient races but make them
easily tamable; let the favored enemies be from a wider background but
don't make the bonuses against them too extensive.

Would the ally and enemy come from a whole class of monsters (the
whole character group) or just a specific one? (e.g. Arch-Lich only or
all L?)

Should there be rules on genociding your enemy? (Or your ally, but why
would you?)

Depending on alignment, would your god be angry/would your alignment
change if you attacked your species ally?

Hope some of that rambling's useful.

